  "E1" [label="(25,0,0) Currently the study creates hyperuniform over-jammed packings, referred to as hyperuniform glasses (HG), using an iterative algorithm that combines particle resizing with energy minimisation at a fixed packing fraction well above the jamming point."];
  "E2" [label="(26,0,0) Currently the density spectrum χv(q) of hyperuniform glasses exhibits ideal power-law scaling with α = 4, categorising them as class I hyperuniformity."];
  "E3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently hyperuniform states are classified by χv(q -> 0) ~ q^α, with class I corresponding to α > 1."];
  "E4" [label="(27,0,0) Currently conventional over-jammed packings, denoted conventional glasses (CG), are produced by quenching the equilibrium liquid state heated from hyperuniform glasses."];
  "E5" [label="(28,0,0) Currently two marginally jammed states are obtained by decompression from hyperuniform glasses and conventional glasses, designated JHG and JCG, with jamming points of ϕ = 0.666 and 0.648, respectively."];
  "E6" [label="(15,0,0) Currently the jamming transition occurs when packing fraction ϕ exceeds the threshold known as the jamming point, ϕJ, at which rigidity emerges."];
  "E7" [label="(16,0,0) Currently marginally jammed states formed by decompressing instantaneously quenched over-jammed packings or compressing ordinary liquids show linear density-spectrum scaling at intermediate wavenumbers, but deviate at lower wavenumbers."];
  "E8" [label="(17,0,0) Currently the low-wavenumber deviation of the density spectrum in marginally jammed states either saturates to a constant or transitions to a new power-law scaling with α ≈ 0.25."];
